course: dictionary
lesson: 13449
title: Hostility: feelings, opposition and war
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Hostility is a noun. It names an unfriendly, angry feeling or behaviour towards someone. «The two families had shown hostility towards each other for years.» The clue is that the families have been at odds for a long time, so hostility means bad feelings between them.
explain: Hostility can also mean strong opposition to an idea or plan. «The council showed hostility to the plan for a new road.» People who oppose a plan do not want it to happen. Ask yourself what word would fit, then put your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: The plural, hostilities, is a formal word for fighting in a war. «When the treaty was signed, the hostilities finally came to an end.» The clue is came to an end, because a war stops, but a feeling does not. So with hostility think about feelings, and with hostilities think about war.
fact: Hostility means unfriendly, angry behaviour or feelings towards someone.
fact: Hostility can also mean strong opposition to an idea or plan.
fact: Hostilities (plural, formal) means fighting in a war.
fact: Useful phrases: open hostility, show hostility, hostility towards, the end of hostilities.
fact: Use the clues: a feeling or attitude is hostility, while a war or fighting is hostilities.
exercise: «The two families had shown hostility towards each other for years.» What does hostility mean here? | *Unfriendly, angry feelings towards someone | Being very tired after a long day | Kind visits and friendly chats | Fighting in a race
exercise: «The council showed hostility to the plan for a new road.» What does hostility show here? | *Strong opposition to the plan | A surprise party | Support and cheering for the plan | Lost interest in roads
exercise: «When the treaty was signed, the hostilities finally came to an end.» What does hostilities mean? | Friendly games in the park | *Fighting in a war | Long, quiet walks | Noisy birthday parties
test: «The crowd greeted the new player with open hostility.» What does open hostility suggest? | *Clear, angry dislike shown for everyone to see | A friendly welcome with cheers | Very quiet and shy | Sleepy and bored
test: Which word is the noun, a name for the feeling? | *hostility | hostile | hostilely
exam: «After years of fighting, both sides agreed to the end of hostilities.» Which phrase is closest in meaning to the end of hostilities? | A start to a long holiday | A long argument about homework | Loud cheering at a football match | *The stopping of fighting
